As a theological thought experiment, you can imagine it that way, but biology doesn’t give us evidence that the clitoris was “placed outside” to enforce a particular sexual code.

Anatomically, the visible clitoris is only the external portion of a much larger internal erectile structure. Its location makes sense developmentally and evolutionarily as part of female sexual anatomy, not as a built-in rule distinguishing “permitted” from “forbidden” sex.

The loyalty piece is cultural rather than anatomical. Different societies and religious traditions have defined infidelity very differently: some focus on penetration, some on exclusivity of sexual contact generally, some on intent, and some historically tolerated plural marriage or concubinage under specific rules.

So the strongest version of your idea is not “the anatomy proves this design,” but something more interesting: humans often take bodily affordances and build moral systems around them after the fact. Anatomy supplies possibilities; culture decides which possibilities count as intimacy, fidelity, violation, or play.